<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="skills" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>List of Fundamental Skills</title>
    <p>
        The following list contains a brief description of the 15
        fundamental skills that will be tested in the class. Each one
        of the skills requires proficiency in other subskills, but the
        brief descriptions should give you an idea of what to expect
        on exams.
    </p>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the concept of relative velocity to reason
                    about kinematics problems using different frames
                    of reference.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can apply Newton's second law in vector form for
                    a single object, including kinematic constraints
                    (e.g circuar motion).
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can apply Newton's second law to a set of coupled
                    objects constrained by ropes or pulleys.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can apply the impulse-momentum relationship to describe
                    sudden processes like collisions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can apply the impulse-momentum relationship to systems
                    that have mass flow (e.g rockets) or momentum flow
                    (e.g a chain falling on the ground)
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can quantitatively describe physical processes using
                    the work-energy theorem, and I can differentiate between
                    real work and center-of-mass work, as defined in class.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can solve problems that include both
                    work-energy and impulse-momentum arguments,
                    like ellastic collisions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can solve rotational statics problems, including
                    cases when the center of mass is accelerated.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can solve planar motion problems that involve applying
                    Newton's second law and the torque-angular momentum relationship
                    in tandem.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can apply conservation of angular momentum to collisions of
                    rigid bodies involving rotation or to single-particle problems
                    involving a central force.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the concepts of time dilation and length contraction
                    to describe observations made by two observers in different
                    inertial reference frames
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the Lorentz transformations to describe observations
                    made by two observers in different reference frames, including
                    the transformation of velocities.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use a spacetime diagram to describe observations made
                    by two observers in different reference frames.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use conservation of 4-momentum to model relativistic
                    collisions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use conservation of 4-momentum to model the decays
                    of unstable particles.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
    
</section>